add(2009,4,4,'If every pair-sum residue were present, they would all be distinct; then all ordered nonzero differences would also be distinct, exceeding the modulus.','A residue avoided by all pair sums',r'For $m\ge3$ integers $a_1,\ldots,a_m$, put $N=m(m+1)/2$. Prove that some integer $k$ is not congruent modulo $N$ to any $a_i+a_j$, allowing $i=j$.',r'There are exactly $N$ unordered index pairs with repetition. If all residues occurred, these pair sums would be pairwise distinct. In particular the $a_i$ are distinct modulo $N$. Moreover the $m(m-1)$ ordered differences $a_i-a_j$ with $i\ne j$ would all be distinct: equality $a_i-a_j=a_k-a_l$ modulo $N$ implies equality of pair sums $a_i+a_l=a_k+a_j$, forcing the same ordered pair. None is zero. But $m(m-1)>N-1$ for $m\ge3$, so these differences cannot fit among the $N-1$ nonzero residues. This contradiction proves the claim.','Combinatorics',['Pigeonhole principle','Counting','Modular arithmetic','Sidon sets'],source=s)
add(2009,5,3.5,'For crossing edges, two strict triangle inequalities contradict the nearest-neighbour choices; the longest edge of any cycle similarly contradicts its choosing endpoint.',source=s)
add(2009,6,4.5,'The nested recursion must be resolved in several boundary intervals before the repeated output block and uniqueness become clear.','A nested recursion on positive integers',r'Find all functions $f:\mathbb N\to\mathbb N$ satisfying $f(n)=n-9$ for $n>2009$ and $f(n)=f(f(n+1009))$ for $n\le2009$.',r'The unique function is $f(n)=n-9$ for $n>2009$, and $f(n)=2001+((n-10)\bmod1000)$ for $1\le n\le2009$, where the remainder is between 0 and 999. To derive it, first take $1010\le n\le2009$: both nested arguments exceed 2009, so $f(n)=n+991$. For $1001\le n\le1009$, the inner value is $n+1000$, now in the preceding interval; hence $f(n)=n+1991$. For $1\le n\le1000$, the inner argument $n+1009$ is in the first interval, so its value is $n+2000$. Applying the already determined function gives $n+2991$ when $n\le9$ and $n+1991$ otherwise. These intervals cover the domain and directly verify the formula.','Algebra',['Functional equations','Recurrences','Case analysis'],source=s)
add(2009,7,4,'A point on the longer side at the length of the shorter side creates an isosceles triangle whose exterior angle equals the standard incenter angle.','Equal chords through the side lines and the incentre',r'Let $I$ be the incentre of triangle $ABC$, with $AC\ne BC$. The circle through $A,I,B$ meets line $AC$ again at $X$ and line $BC$ again at $Y$. Prove $AX=BY$.',r'Assume $AC=b>BC=a$; the other case is symmetric. Choose $X_0$ on $CA$ with $CX_0=a$. Triangle $CBX_0$ is isosceles, so $\angle AX_0B=90^\circ+\angle C/2=\angle AIB$. Hence $A,B,I,X_0$ are concyclic, and $X=X_0$. Power of $C$ with respect to this circle gives $CA\cdot CX=CB\cdot CY$, with directed lengths; therefore $CY=b$. Thus $Y$ is on ray $CB$ beyond $B$, and $AX=b-a=BY$.','Geometry',['Incentre','Cyclic quadrilaterals','Isosceles triangles','Power of a point'],source=s)
