add(2022,3,5.5,'The circle tangency gives a homothety, and two non-obvious angle-bisector products show it carries an excentre precisely onto BC.','Two external bisectors on the third side',r'Let $I$ be the incentre of triangle $ABC$. A circle through $I$ is tangent to the circumcircle at $A$ and meets $AB,AC$ again at $P,Q$. Prove that the angle bisectors of $\angle BPQ$ and $\angle PQC$ meet on $BC$.',r'Let $S$ be the second intersection of $AI$ with the circumcircle, let $R=AI\cap BC$, and let $E$ be the excentre opposite $A$. The tangent circles are related by the homothety centred at $A$ with ratio $AI/AS$, which carries $B,C,S$ to $P,Q,I$. Standard sine-rule formulas for the angle bisector give $AR\cdot AS=AB\cdot AC$: if $b=AC,c=AB$, then $AR=2bc\cos(A/2)/(b+c)$ and $AS=(b+c)/(2\cos(A/2))$. Also $AI\cdot AE=bc$, since $AI=r/\sin(A/2)$, $AE=r_a/\sin(A/2)$, $rr_a=\Delta^2/[s(s-a)]$ and $\sin^2(A/2)=(s-b)(s-c)/(bc)$, together with Heron’s formula. Therefore $AR/AE=AI/AS$. The same homothety sends $E$ to $R$, making $R$ the excentre of $APQ$ opposite $A$. Its two external bisectors are exactly those requested.','Geometry',['Homothety','Incentre','Excentre','Angle bisectors','Circles'],competition=c,source='https://enrichedmaths.maths.uwa.edu.au/Olympiad/SMC/SMC2022wsoln.pdf')
